// Whether the camera can see this face, and the shading that follows.
//
// A face is visible when the eye is on the front side of its plane:
// `n . (eye - v0) > 0`. That is the same question the rasteriser answers from the
// screen winding, asked several thousand operations earlier -- before the
// vertices are transformed, before the near plane is clipped, before anything is
// projected, and before the square root that shades it. The rasteriser's own test
// still runs and is still authoritative; this one only *skips* work.
//
// Zero is kept rather than culled: an exactly edge-on face is one the winding
// test drops anyway, and deciding it here would only be a second opinion.
//
// The dot needs a word on both sides. The normal reaches 1.6e9 and a room's
// coordinates reach 5e4 millunits, so one term is 8e13.

bl_vis(int[] tri) {
  if (bl_front(tri) >= 0) {
    bl_shade(tri);
  }
} return void;

// Quartered on the way into the lighting, which is where the components have to
// fit an int and be squared: 4e8 each, so the sum of squares is 4.8e17 and its
// root 6.9e8, both comfortably inside what fx_hyp3 can carry. The scale divides
// out of the cosine, so quartering here changes nothing but the range.
bl_shade(int[] tri) {
  int nvx = (import bl_e)[0] / 4;
  int nvy = (import bl_e)[1] / 4;
  bl_shade2(tri, nvx, nvy);
} return void;
